['```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Deserializer {
    private final ExternalService service;
    private final Map<String, Class<?>> classCache = new HashMap<>();

    public Deserializer(ExternalService service) {
        this.service = service;
    }

    public Object deserialize(byte[] bytes) {
        try {
            String className = getClassName(bytes);
            Class<?> clazz = classCache.get(className);
            if (clazz == null) {
                // Resolve class metadata dynamically from the external service
                clazz = service.resolveClass(className);
                classCache.put(className, clazz);
            }

            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes));
            return ois.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            // Handle deserialization errors gracefully and prevent system-wide failures
            System.err.println("Deserialization error: " + e);
            return null;
        }
    }

    private String getClassName(byte[] bytes) {
        try {
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes));
            return ois.readUTF();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Handle errors gracefully and prevent system-wide failures
            System.err.println("Error reading class name: " + e);
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```
The `Deserializer` class takes an `ExternalService` instance in its constructor, which is used to dynamically resolve class metadata. It also maintains a cache of previously resolved classes to reduce the number of requests made to the external service.

The `deserialize` method first attempts to read the class name from the byte array using `readUTF()`. If this fails, it falls back on resolving the class dynamically using the external service and caching the result for future use.

To handle deserialization errors gracefully and prevent system-wide failures, the method catches all exceptions and logs them to standard error. It also returns a null object to indicate that an error occurred during deserialization.

The `Deserializer` class is designed to be thread-safe by using a cache of previously resolved classes. This allows multiple threads to access the same `Deserializer` instance concurrently without causing conflicts.

To optimize performance and reduce memory usage, the `deserialize` method uses an `ObjectInputStream` to read the object data from the byte array directly, rather than first creating a temporary byte array using `ByteArrayOutputStream`. This avoids the need for additional buffering and copying of data.

The `getClassName` method is used to extract the class name from the byte array without deserializing the entire object. It uses an `ObjectInputStream` to read the class name in UTF-8 format, which is more efficient than using `ByteArrayOutputStream`.

To test the `Deserializer` class, unit tests and integration tests can be implemented to cover typical usage patterns, edge cases, and error scenarios. For example:
