## Example of using curl_easy_perform()

In this example, we will show you how to make a HTTP request using the `curl_easy_perform()` function.

First, you need to initialize the CURL library by calling the `curl_global_init()` function:
```c
curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_ALL);
```
Then, you can set up your HTTP request using the `curl_easy_setopt()` function. For example, if you want to make a GET request to a specific URL, you would use the following code:
```c
CURL *curl;
CURLcode res;

curl = curl_easy_init();
if(curl) {
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, "http://www.example.com");
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_VERBOSE, 1L);
  res = curl_easy_perform(curl);
  if(res != CURLE_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, "curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\n", curl_easy_strerror(res));
  }
  curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
}
```
The `CURLOPT_URL` option specifies the URL you want to make a request to, and the `CURLOPT_VERBOSE` option enables verbose mode, which will display detailed information about the HTTP request. The `res` variable contains the result of the `curl_easy_perform()` function, and if it is not equal to `CURLE_OK`, an error occurred during the request.

After you have finished making your request, you can clean up the CURL handle using the `curl_easy_cleanup()` function:
```c
curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
```
## Example of using curl_global_init() and curl_global_cleanup()

In this example, we will show you how to initialize and clean up the CURL library using the `curl_global_init()` and `curl_global_cleanup()` functions.

First, you need to initialize the CURL library by calling the `curl_global_init()` function:
```c
CURLcode res;

res = curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_ALL);
if(res != CURLE_OK) {
  fprintf(stderr, "curl_global_init() failed: %s\n", curl_easy_strerror(res));
}
```
The `CURL_GLOBAL_ALL` option specifies that the CURL library should be initialized with all available features. The `res` variable contains the result of the `curl_global_init()` function, and if it is not equal to `CURLE_OK`, an error occurred during initialization.

After you have finished making your requests, you can clean up the CURL library by calling the `curl_global_cleanup()` function:
```c
res = curl_global_cleanup();
if(res != CURLE_OK) {
  fprintf(stderr, "curl_global_cleanup() failed: %s\n", curl_easy_strerror(res));
}
```
This will release any resources used by the CURL library.']

