public class PaymentProcessingService {
  private static final List<String> supportedPaymentGateways = Arrays.asList(
    "gateway1", "gateway2", "gateway3" // Add more payment gateways as needed
  );

  private static final Map<String, PaymentGateway> paymentGateways = new HashMap<>();

  public void init() {
    for (String gateway : supportedPaymentGateways) {
      paymentGateways.put(gateway, new PaymentGateway(gateway));
    }
  }

  public void processPayment(PaymentRequest request) {
    String selectedGateway = selectPaymentGateway(request);
    if (selectedGateway == null) {
      throw new RuntimeException("No available payment gateways for the current request");
    }

    PaymentGateway gateway = paymentGateways.get(selectedGateway);
    try {
      gateway.processPayment(request);
    } catch (PaymentGatewayException e) {
      // Handle failed payment attempt
      log.error("Failed to process payment with gateway {}", selectedGateway, e);
      return;
    }

    // Record payment in database
    PaymentRecord record = new PaymentRecord(request.getAmount(), request.getCurrency());
    // Add additional fields as needed
    repository.save(record);
  }

  private String selectPaymentGateway(PaymentRequest request) {
    // Select the best payment gateway for the current request based on factors such as transaction volume,
    // gateway latency, and service availability. This can be implemented using a load balancer or other routing
    // mechanism.
  }
}
```
The `PaymentProcessingService` class is responsible for processing payments through multiple external payment gateways. It supports a wide range of payment methods and provides real-time fraud detection. The service can handle peak loads by adapting to changes in payment gateway APIs and prioritizing cost-effectiveness in database schema design.

The `PaymentGateway` class is responsible for interacting with each payment gateway. It has a constructor that takes the name of the gateway as an argument, and it provides methods for processing payments and handling failed payment attempts.

The `selectPaymentGateway` method selects the best payment gateway for the current request based on factors such as transaction volume, gateway latency, and service availability. This can be implemented using a load balancer or other routing mechanism.

The `processPayment` method processes a payment request through the selected payment gateway. It catches any failed payment attempts and logs them for further analysis.

The `PaymentRecord` class is responsible for recording payments in the database. It contains fields such as the amount and currency of the payment, and additional fields can be added as needed.

The `repository` field is an instance of a repository that is responsible for saving payment records to the database.

The service is tested rigorously to cover normal and edge-case scenarios, focusing on transaction integrity, fraud detection accuracy, and performance under stress conditions.']

