Here is the code block for the fault-tolerant, scalable, and extensible configuration management system:
```java
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class ConfigManager {
    private final Map<String, Object> config = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    public void loadConfig(String fileName) throws Exception {
        // Load the configuration from an external JSON file
        String jsonConfig = readFile(fileName);
        Map<String, Object> configData = parseJSON(jsonConfig);
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            config.clear();
            config.putAll(configData);
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public void updateConfig(String key, Object value) throws Exception {
        // Update the configuration value for a given key
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            config.put(key, value);
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public Object getConfig(String key) throws Exception {
        // Get the configuration value for a given key
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            return config.get(key);
        } finally {
            lock.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public void close() {
        // Release resources
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            config.clear();
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}
```
This code block implements a `ConfigManager` class that manages configuration data in a `ConcurrentHashMap`. The class provides methods for loading, updating, and retrieving configuration values from an external JSON file. It also includes a lock mechanism to ensure thread-safe access to the configuration data.

The `loadConfig()` method loads the configuration from an external JSON file and updates the `config` map with the new values. The method acquires a write lock on the `lock` object to prevent concurrent access while updating the configuration data.

The `updateConfig()` method updates the configuration value for a given key by acquiring a write lock on the `lock` object and updating the `config` map with the new value.

The `getConfig()` method retrieves the configuration value for a given key by acquiring a read lock on the `lock` object and returning the corresponding value from the `config` map.

The `close()` method releases resources by clearing the `config` map and releasing the write lock on the `lock` object.

This code block can be easily integrated with third-party services for real-time updates, using dependency injection frameworks to manage configuration dependencies and custom provider plugins for seamless integration with existing infrastructure.']

