```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.concurrent.BasicThreadFactory;

public class RealTimeNotificationSystem {
  private final ExecutorService executor;
  private final Set<ExternalService> externalServices;
  private final Map<String, UserData> userData;
  private final RateLimiter rateLimiter;

  public RealTimeNotificationSystem() {
    // Create an executor service for handling incoming requests
    executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool(
      new BasicThreadFactory.Builder().build());

    // Initialize the set of external services and user data
    externalServices = new HashSet<>();
    userData = new HashMap<>();

    // Create a rate limiter to enforce limits on external service invocations
    rateLimiter = new RateLimiter(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
  }

  public void registerExternalService(String name) {
    // Add the new external service to the set of registered services
    ExternalService service = new ExternalService(name);
    externalServices.add(service);

    // Initialize the user data map for the new service
    Map<String, UserData> serviceUserData = new HashMap<>();
    userData.put(service, serviceUserData);
  }

  public void unregisterExternalService(String name) {
    // Remove the external service from the set of registered services
    ExternalService service = new ExternalService(name);
    externalServices.remove(service);

    // Remove the user data map for the removed service
    userData.remove(service);
  }

  public void notifyUser(String userId, String message) {
    // Check if the user has registered with any external services
    UserData userData = getUserData(userId);
    if (userData == null) {
      return;
    }

    // Notify all registered external services for the user
    for (ExternalService service : externalServices) {
      notifyExternalService(service, userId, message);
    }
  }

  private UserData getUserData(String userId) {
    // Return the user data map for the specified user ID
    return userData.get(userId);
  }

  private void notifyExternalService(ExternalService service, String userId, String message) {
    // Check if the rate limiter allows invocation of the external service
    if (!rateLimiter.tryAcquire()) {
      return;
    }

    // Invoke the external service with the specified user ID and message
    try {
      service.sendNotification(userId, message);
    } catch (Exception e) {
      // Handle any errors that occur during invocation of the external service
    }
  }
}
```
This code block defines a `RealTimeNotificationSystem` class that manages real-time notifications for multiple external services. The class has the following methods:

* `registerExternalService`: Adds an external service to the set of registered services.
* `unregisterExternalService`: Removes an external service from the set of registered services.
* `notifyUser`: Notifies all registered external services for a specific user ID with a given message.

The class also defines several private methods for managing user data and rate limiting invocations of external services. The `executor` field is used to handle incoming requests asynchronously, while the `externalServices`, `userData`, and `rateLimiter` fields are used to manage the set of registered external services, user data maps, and rate limits for each service, respectively.

The `notifyUser` method first retrieves the user data map for the specified user ID using the `getUserData` method. If no user data is found, the method returns without taking any further action. Otherwise, it iterates over all registered external services and invokes the `notifyExternalService` method for each service, passing in the user ID and message to be sent. The `notifyExternalService` method first checks if the rate limiter allows an invocation of the external service using the `tryAcquire` method. If the limiter does not allow the invocation, the method returns without taking any further action. Otherwise, it attempts to invoke the external service with the specified user ID and message, handling any errors that may occur during invocation using a `try-catch` block.']

