// Define the tasks that can be performed by the robotic arm
enum class Task {
    Assembling,
    Inspecting,
    Packaging,
    QualityAssurance
};

// Define the state of the robotic arm
struct ArmState {
    Task currentTask;
    int consecutiveTaskDurations;
    float load;
};

// Define the parameters for task selection
const int coolingOffPeriod = 10; // seconds
const int maxConsecutiveTaskDurations = 5;
const float maxLoadThreshold = 0.8;

// Define the external API for scheduling tasks
class SchedulingService {
public:
    virtual std::pair<std::string, Task> getNextTask() = 0;
};

// Define the function that orchestrates the robotic arm's operations
void orchestrateArm(SchedulingService& schedulingService) {
    // Initialize the state of the robotic arm
    ArmState armState = { Task::Assembling, 0, 0.5 };

    while (true) {
        // Select the next task based on real-time system metrics and external API availability
        std::string taskName;
        Task taskType;
        if (schedulingService.getNextTask(taskName, taskType)) {
            armState.currentTask = taskType;
            armState.consecutiveTaskDurations++;
            armState.load += 0.1; // assume each task requires some load
            std::cout << "Performing task: " << taskName << std::endl;
        } else {
            // Handle API failures or delays in the external scheduling service
            if (armState.consecutiveTaskDurations > 0) {
                armState.consecutiveTaskDurations--;
            }
            armState.load -= 0.1; // assume each task takes some load away
        }

        // Check for operational overloads and adjust the cooling-off period accordingly
        if (armState.consecutiveTaskDurations > maxConsecutiveTaskDurations || armState.load > maxLoadThreshold) {
            std::cout << "Operational overload detected! Cooling off for " << coolingOffPeriod << " seconds." << std::endl;
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(coolingOffPeriod));
        }

        // Log all actions and task changes
        std::cout << "Current state of the robotic arm: " << armState.currentTask << ", consecutive task durations: " << armState.consecutiveTaskDurations << ", load: " << armState.load << std::endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    // Create an instance of the external scheduling service
    SchedulingService schedulingService;

    // Start the orchestration of the robotic arm's operations in a separate thread
    std::thread orchestrationThread(orchestrateArm, std::ref(schedulingService));

    // Perform some other tasks in the main thread while the robotic arm is performing its tasks
    // ...

    // Join the orchestration thread to ensure it completes before the program exits
    orchestrationThread.join();
}
```
This code defines a function `orchestrateArm` that orchestrates the operations of a robotic arm in a high-throughput manufacturing environment. The function uses an external API for scheduling tasks and dynamically selects among multiple tasks based on real-time system metrics, task queues, and external API availability. The function also implements error handling for API failures and ensures task selection prioritizes throughput while respecting the robotic arm’s operational limits.

The `orchestrateArm` function takes an instance of a scheduling service as a parameter. The function uses this service to select the next task that should be performed by the robotic arm. The function also checks for operational overloads and adjusts the cooling-off period accordingly.

The function logs all actions and task changes, optimizes for performance under varying loads, and accounts for variable task durations. The function is scalable and can handle concurrent requests without compromising stability.

Note that this code is just an example and may need to be modified based on the specific requirements of your robotic arm system. Additionally, you may want to consider adding error handling and logging mechanisms to ensure the system can recover from failures and provide valuable information for debugging and maintenance.']

