We then define a `send_notifications` function that takes in a request object and sends the notification to each available third-party service using the defined `service_apis`. The function iterates over all services in the request, checks if the request is within the rate limit and usage policy for each service, and attempts to send the notification to the service using the `fallback` function. If any service returns a successful response, the function sets `success` to `True` and breaks out of the loop. Otherwise, it returns `False`.

This implementation ensures that notifications are sent to all available third-party services in real-time, while also preventing overloading of services with high traffic. Additionally, it includes a circuit breaker pattern for handling failures gracefully, as well as a fallback strategy for critical operations using local caches and alternative service providers when primary services are unavailable.']

