}

// Function to deserialize an entire UE context from a byte array for seamless integration with an external RRC protocol stack
int deserializeUEContext(UEContextArray *array, uint16_t UE_ID, char *serialized_context) {
    // Find the index of the UE context in the array
    int index = -1;
    for (int i = 0; i < array->num_elements; i++) {
        if (array->data[i]->UE_ID == UE_ID) {
            index = i;
            break;
        }
    }

    // Check if the UE context was found in the array
    if (index < 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Deserialize the UE context structure
    LTE_PHY_RRC_UE_CONTEXT *context = array->data[index];
    memcpy(context, serialized_context, sizeof(LTE_PHY_RRC_UE_CONTEXT));

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation defines a `UEContextArray` structure to represent a dynamic array of `LTE_PHY_RRC_UE_CONTEXT` structures. The `addNewUEContext`, `removeUEContext`, and `modifyUEContext` functions provide thread-safe access to the array for adding, removing, and modifying UE contexts, respectively. The `serializeUEContext` and `deserializeUEContext` functions are used to serialize and deserialize entire UE contexts, respectively, for seamless integration with an external RRC protocol stack.

The implementation also includes a `RRC_STATE` enumeration to represent the different states of a UE in the LTE network. The `modifyUEContext` function uses this enumeration to modify the RRC state of a UE context in the array.

This is just one possible implementation of a dynamic array data structure for managing UE contexts in an LTE network. There are many variations and optimizations that could be made to improve performance and reduce memory usage, depending on the specific requirements of the system.']

