    // Method for removing a node from the distributed store
    void removeNode(int index) {
        // Lock the data structures and synchronize access
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);

        // Remove the node at the given index in the set of nodes in the distributed store
        if (index >= 0 && index < nodes.size()) {
            nodes.erase(nodes.begin() + index);
        }
    }

    // Method for clearing all key-value pairs from the distributed store
    void clearKeyValuePairs() {
        // Lock the data structures and synchronize access
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);

        // Clear all key-value pairs in the map of key-value pairs in the distributed store
        keyValuePairs.clear();
    }

    // Method for clearing all nodes from the distributed store
    void clearNodes() {
        // Lock the data structures and synchronize access
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);

        // Clear all nodes in the set of nodes in the distributed store
        nodes.clear();
    }

    // Method for getting the size of the key-value pairs in the distributed store
    int getKeyValuePairsSize() {
        // Lock the data structures and synchronize access
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);

        // Return the number of key-value pairs in the map of key-value pairs in the distributed store
        return keyValuePairs.size();
    }

    // Method for getting the size of the nodes in the distributed store
    int getNodesSize() {
        // Lock the data structures and synchronize access
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);

        // Return the number of nodes in the set of nodes in the distributed store
        return nodes.size();
    }
};
```
In this example, we have defined a `DistributedStore` class that manages two data structures: a map of key-value pairs and a set of nodes. The class provides methods for adding, removing, updating, and checking the presence of key-value pairs and nodes in the distributed store. The class also provides methods for getting the size of the key-value pairs and nodes in the distributed store.

The `DistributedStore` class uses a mutex to synchronize access to the data structures, ensuring that only one thread can modify them at a time. This is important because multiple threads may try to access the same data structure simultaneously, potentially causing inconsistencies or errors.

We have also used smart pointers to manage memory for the nodes in the distributed store. This is necessary because the nodes are dynamically allocated and need to be freed when they are no longer needed. Smart pointers can automatically manage the memory for us, ensuring that we do not forget to free it.

The `DistributedStore` class can be used to build a distributed key-value store system where multiple clients can access and modify the same data simultaneously. The class provides a thread-safe interface for managing the data structures, ensuring that the data remains consistent across all clients.']

