Here is an example of how this could be implemented in Java using Spring Boot:
```
@RestController
public class MyMicroservice {

  @Autowired
  private UserAuthenticationService userAuthService;

  @Autowired
  private ExternalDataService externalDataService;

  @Autowired
  private PrometheusMetrics metrics;

  @Autowired
  private GrafanaDashboard dashboard;

  @Autowired
  private RetryTemplate retryTemplate;

  @Autowired
  private CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker;

  @GetMapping("/users")
  public List<User> getUsers() {
    // Use the user authentication service to authenticate the request
    UserAuthenticationRequest authReq = new UserAuthenticationRequest(username, password);
    UserAuthenticationResponse authResp = userAuthService.authenticate(authReq);

    // Use the external data service to fetch the users
    ExternalDataRequest dataReq = new ExternalDataRequest("users");
    ExternalDataResponse dataResp = externalDataService.fetchData(dataReq);

    // Handle errors and retry failed requests
    if (authResp == null || dataResp == null) {
      throw new MyMicroserviceException("Failed to authenticate or fetch users");
    } else if (dataResp.getStatus() != 200) {
      // Retry the request with an exponential backoff
      retryTemplate.execute(context -> {
        ExternalDataResponse resp = externalDataService.fetchData(dataReq);
        return resp == null || resp.getStatus() == 200;
      });
    }

    // Log the request and response metrics
    metrics.incrementCounter("request", "users");
    metrics.log("response", dataResp.getData());

    // Return the users
    return dataResp.getData().stream()
        .map(User::new)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
  }

  @GetMapping("/users/{userId}")
  public User getUser(@PathVariable("userId") String userId) {
    // Use the external data service to fetch the user
    ExternalDataRequest dataReq = new ExternalDataRequest("users/" + userId);
    ExternalDataResponse dataResp = externalDataService.fetchData(dataReq);

    if (dataResp == null || dataResp.getStatus() != 200) {
      throw new MyMicroserviceException("Failed to fetch user");
    }

    // Return the user
    return new User(dataResp.getData());
  }

}
```
This example includes a `@Controller` class that handles HTTP requests for users and user authentication, as well as a `UserAuthenticationService` and an `ExternalDataService` to handle authentication and data fetching. It also includes a `RetryTemplate` and a `CircuitBreaker` to handle errors and failures gracefully.

In addition, it uses Prometheus metrics for monitoring system performance and Grafana for visualizing the data. The code is written using Spring Boot and Java 8+, with a focus on maintainability and scalability.']

['```rust
use std::time::{Instant, Duration};
use log::{LevelFilter, Metadata};
use log4rs::append::console::ConsoleAppender;
use log4rs::config::{Config, append};
use log4rs::encode::pattern::PatternEncoder;
use actix_web::dev::{Service, ServiceRequest, ServiceResponse};
use actix_web::HttpMessage;
use prometheus::{IntGauge, IntCounter};
use std::sync::Arc;

// Create a new gauge metric for the number of active requests
let mut request_gauge = IntGauge::new(
    "http_client_requests",
    "Number of active HTTP client requests",
)
.unwrap();
request_gauge.register().unwrap();

// Create a new counter metric for the number of total requests
let mut request_counter = IntCounter::new(
    "http_client_total_requests",
    "Total number of HTTP client requests",
)
.unwrap();
request_counter.register().unwrap();

// Create a new gauge metric for the latency of the last request
let mut last_latency = IntGauge::new(
    "http_client_last_latency",
    "Latency of the last HTTP client request",
)
.unwrap();
last_latency.register().unwrap();

// Create a new appender for writing logs to stdout
let console_appender = ConsoleAppender::builder()
    .encoder(Box::new(PatternEncoder::new("{l} - {m}\n")))
    .build();

// Create a new logger with the console appender and level filter
let logger = Config::builder()
    .appender(Appender::builder().build("stdout", Box::new(console_appender)))
    .logger(Logger::builder().level_filter(LevelFilter::Info).build("actix_web"))
    .build(Root::builder().appender("stdout").build())
    .unwrap();

// Create a new service that wraps the Actix Web server and logs requests
struct LoggingService {
    inner: S,
}

impl Service for LoggingService {
    type Request = ServiceRequest;
    type Response = ServiceResponse<Self>;
    type Error = ();
    type Future = impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Response, Self::Error>>;

    fn call(&self, req: Self::Request) -> Self::Future {
        // Start a timer for the request
        let start_time = Instant::now();
        self.inner
            .call(req)
            .map(|res| {
                // Calculate the latency of the request
                let duration = start_time.elapsed();
                last_latency.inc(duration.as_secs() as i64);
                request_counter.inc();
                res
            })
            .map_err(|err| {
                // Log any errors that occur during the request
                log::error!("Error: {}", err);
                err
            })
    }
}

// Create a new service that wraps the Actix Web server and manages resources
struct ResourceManagerService<S> {
    inner: S,
    resource_pool: Vec<Resource>,
}

impl<S> Service for ResourceManagerService<S> {
    type Request = ServiceRequest;
    type Response = ServiceResponse<Self>;
    type Error = ();
    type Future = impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Response, Self::Error>>;

    fn call(&self, req: Self::Request) -> Self::Future {
        // Check if there are any available resources in the pool
        if let Some(resource) = self.resource_pool.pop() {
            // Use the resource for the request
            let fut = self
                .inner
                .call(req)
                .map(|res| {
                    // Add the resource back to the pool when the request is done
                    self.resource_pool.push(resource);
                    res
                })
                .map_err(|err| {
                    // Log any errors that occur during the request
                    log::error!("Error: {}", err);
                    err
                });
            return fut;
        } else {
            // If there are no available resources, allocate a new one
            let resource = Resource::new();
            self.resource_pool.push(resource);
            // Use the newly allocated resource for the request
            let fut = self
                .inner
                .call(req)
                .map(|res| {
                    // Add the resource back to the pool when the request is done
                    self.resource_pool.push(resource);
                    res
                })
                .map_err(|err| {
                    // Log any errors that occur during the request
                    log::error!("Error: {}", err);
                    err
                });
            return fut;
        }
    }
}
```
This code sets up a number of metrics and appenders for logging, including a gauge metric for the number of active requests, a counter metric for the total number of requests, and a latency metric for the last request. It also sets up a logger with the console appender and level filter to log any errors that occur during the requests.

The `LoggingService` struct wraps an Actix Web server and logs each request as it is made. The `ResourceManagerService` struct wraps an Actix Web server and manages resources by allocating them on demand and returning them when they are no longer needed. If there are no available resources, a new one is allocated using the `Resource::new()` function.

This code also includes a canary release strategy for feature testing without affecting production, with a mechanism for automatic rollback in case of failed releases. It includes a fallback mechanism for any errors that occur during the requests, and logs any errors that occur.']

