This code defines a `NotificationService` class that handles sending notifications to multiple external APIs, including event streaming platforms (ESPs), message queuing services (MQSs), and third-party notification systems (NSs). The service has several features such as retry logic, circuit breaking, secure credential management, and comprehensive logging and monitoring capabilities.

The constructor of the class takes in a list of ESP URLs, MQS URLs, and NS URLs as inputs, which are used to initialize the service with the appropriate endpoints for each type of API. The `send_notification` method sends a message to all three types of APIs, and it retries failed attempts up to 5 times before raising an exception. If any of the APIs fail, the service will continue to retry until it succeeds or reaches the maximum number of retries.

The service also uses a circuit breaker mechanism to detect temporary failures and prevent cascading failures. If a failure is detected, the service will wait for a specified amount of time (30 seconds in this case) before trying again. The `retry_count` variable tracks the number of retries that have been attempted, and it is reset back to 5 after each successful notification.

The service also implements message deduplication and idempotency to ensure reliable delivery of notifications even under high-latency conditions. It uses a queue to store messages until they are sent successfully, and it sets the `message_id` header for each message to prevent duplicate delivery.

Finally, the service provides comprehensive logging and monitoring capabilities to facilitate troubleshooting and performance tuning. It uses a distributed tracing system to monitor message flow across different services, and it stores logs in a persistent storage solution such as a database or file system. The service also includes error handling for all external API calls, including retries with exponential backoff and circuit breaking.']

