### 4.2 Scaling the application horizontally

In order to scale the application horizontally, we need to add more instances of the same container running the same code. We can do this by creating a replica set in Kubernetes. A replica set is a set of replica pods that are created and managed automatically based on a desired number of replicas.

Here's an example of how we can create a replica set for our application:
```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: example-replicaset
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: example-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: example-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: example-container
        image: docker.io/example/example-image:latest
```
This replica set will create three instances of the `example-container` running the same code as the previous deployment, and they will be managed automatically based on the desired number of replicas (in this case 3).

### 4.3 Scaling the application vertically

In order to scale the application vertically, we need to increase the resources allocated to each instance of the container running the code. We can do this by changing the `resources` field in the deployment configuration. For example:
```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: example-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: example-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: example-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: example-container
        image: docker.io/example/example-image:latest
        resources:
          limits:
            cpu: "2"
            memory: 4Gi
```
This deployment will create three instances of the `example-container` running the same code as the previous deployment, and each instance will have access to at least 2 CPU cores and 4GB of RAM.

### 4.4 Deploying a service

A Kubernetes service is a logical abstraction over a set of pods. It allows us to expose our application to the outside world through a stable IP address and DNS name.

Here's an example of how we can create a service for our application:
```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: example-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: example-app
  ports:
  - protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 9376
```
This service will select all pods with the label `app: example-app` and expose them through port 80 of a stable IP address. We can also specify the `targetPort` field to direct traffic to a specific port on each pod. In this case, we are exposing port 9376 on each pod as port 80.

### 4.5 Deploying a persistent volume

A Kubernetes persistent volume (PV) is a piece of storage that lives beyond the lifetime of a single pod. We can use PVs to store data that we want to persist between restarts or migrations.

